If it's a continuous random variable, if x is continuous,

261
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:18,500
then the expectation of x is an integral of the probability

262
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:22,500
density of x times x dx.

263
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:25,140
I'm just writing that down for you now, because it's for a

264
00:14:25,140 --> 00:14:27,960
complete list, but I'm not going to explain or elaborate

265
00:14:27,960 --> 00:14:29,940
that.

266
00:14:29,940 --> 00:14:36,760
But basically, these two formulas here are measures of

267
00:14:36,760 --> 00:14:39,960
the central tendency of x.

268
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:50,240
It's essentially the average of x in the probability metric

269
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:52,300
that we have up here.

270
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:56,560
But this formula is something we use to estimate the expected

271
00:14:56,560 --> 00:14:58,120
value of x.

272
00:14:58,120 --> 00:15:02,220
And it says that, and this is called the mean or average,

273
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:07,100
which you've learned long ago, if you have n observations on

274
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:11,780
a random variable x, you can take the sum of the x

275
00:15:11,780 --> 00:15:12,000
observations.

276
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,500
The summation i equals 1 to n, and then divide that by n.

277
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:18,140
That's called the average.

278
00:15:18,140 --> 00:15:23,180
So, what I want to say is that this is the average or the mean

279
00:15:23,180 --> 00:15:27,400
or sample mean, when you have a sample of n observations,

280
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:31,620
which is an estimate of the expected value of x.

281
00:15:31,620 --> 00:15:35,580
So, for example, if we're evaluating an investor who has

282
00:15:35,580 --> 00:15:39,880
invested money, you can get n observations, let's say n

283
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:41,380
annual returns.

284
00:15:41,380 --> 00:15:43,280
And you can take an average of them.

285
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:47,520
And that's the first and most obvious metric representing the

286
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:53,620
success of the investment, if x is the return.

287
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:55,380
People are always wanting to know, they're looking at

288
00:15:55,380 --> 00:15:59,220
someone who invests money, is this person a success or not?

289
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:01,300
Well, this is the first and most obvious measure.

290
00:16:01,300 --> 00:16:03,420
Let's see what the person did on average.

291
00:16:03,420 --> 00:16:07,440
You were investing for, let's say, n equals 10, 10 years.

292
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:10,820
Let's take the returns you made each year, add them up, and

293
00:16:10,820 --> 00:16:11,160
multiply them.

294
00:16:11,160 --> 00:16:14,660
And then you multiply them up and divide by 10.

295
00:16:14,660 --> 00:16:17,040
And that gives us an average.

296
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:21,340
I put this formula down as an alternative, because it's

297
00:16:21,340 --> 00:16:24,720
another, this is called the geometric mean.

298
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:26,940
This is the arithmetic mean.

299
00:16:26,940 --> 00:16:28,340
This is the geometric mean.

300
00:16:28,340 --> 00:16:31,400
And you're probably not so familiar with that, because

301
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:33,500
it's a different concept.

302
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:38,160
The geometric mean, instead of adding your n observations,

303
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:39,640
you multiply them together.

304
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:40,600
You form a product of them.

305
00:16:40,600 --> 00:16:45,840
And then, instead of dividing by n, you take the n-th root of

306
00:16:45,840 --> 00:16:47,020
the product.

307
00:16:47,020 --> 00:16:50,780
And so, that's a formula that's used to estimate the

308
00:16:50,780 --> 00:16:55,600
average return of a portfolio of investments, where we use

309
00:16:55,600 --> 00:17:02,080
gross return for x, not just the simple return.

310
00:17:02,080 --> 00:17:07,400
This geometric mean makes sense only when all the x's

311
00:17:07,400 --> 00:17:09,320
are non-negative.

312
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:10,480
If you put in a negative value, you get a negative value.

313
00:17:10,480 --> 00:17:12,100
You might get a negative product.

314
00:17:12,100 --> 00:17:15,500
Then, if you took the n-th root of that, it would be an

315
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:16,860
imaginary number.

316
00:17:16,860 --> 00:17:17,920
So, let's forget that.

317
00:17:17,920 --> 00:17:20,600
We're not going to apply this formula, if there's any

318
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:22,720
negative numbers.

319
00:17:22,720 --> 00:17:26,420
But it's often used, and I recommend its use, in

320
00:17:26,420 --> 00:17:28,580
evaluating investments.

321
00:17:28,580 --> 00:17:32,840
Because if you use gross return, it gives a better

322
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:35,800
measure of the outcome of the investment.

323
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:36,920
So, think of it this way.

324
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:40,360
Suppose you invested money with some investment manager.

325
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:44,420
And the guy said, I've done a wonderful job of investing

326
00:17:44,420 --> 00:17:45,420
your money.

327
00:17:45,420 --> 00:17:49,260
I made 50% one year, I made 30% another year.

328
00:17:49,260 --> 00:17:55,960
Oh, and by the way, I had one bad year, it was minus 100%.

329
00:17:55,960 --> 00:17:57,600
So what do you think of this investor?

330
00:17:57,600 --> 00:17:59,520
Well, you think about it.

331
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:04,100
If he made 50% one year, and then 30% another year, and then

332
00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:08,220
he lost everything.

333
00:18:08,220 --> 00:18:10,000
That dominates everything, right?

334
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:13,440
If you have a minus 100% simple return, your gross

335
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:15,760
return is zero, OK?

336
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:19,980
So, if I plug in, if I put in a zero here to any of the x's,

337
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:24,340
right, this product will be zero.

338
00:18:24,340 --> 00:18:26,560
Anything times zero is zero.

339
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:28,560
And I take the n-th root of zero, and what's that?

340
00:18:28,560 --> 00:18:30,000
It's zero.

341
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:35,580
So, if there's ever a year in which the return is minus 100%,

342
00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:39,540
then the geometric mean is zero.

343
00:18:39,540 --> 00:18:40,880
That's a good discipline.

344
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:44,280
This obviously doesn't make sense as a way to evaluate

345
00:18:44,280 --> 00:18:45,480
investment success.

346
00:18:45,480 --> 00:18:47,000
Are you following with me on this?

347
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:51,820
Because you care a lot if the guy wipes you out.

348
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:55,180
Whatever else is done after that doesn't matter.

349
00:18:55,180 --> 00:19:01,500
So, that's why we want to use the geometric return.

350
00:19:01,500 --> 00:19:05,960
So, these are all measures of central tendency.

351
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:09,260
That is, what is the central result?

352
00:19:09,540 --> 00:19:11,720
Sometimes the investor had a good year, sometimes the

353
00:19:11,720 --> 00:19:13,360
investor had a bad year.

354
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:16,780
But what was the typical or central value?

355
00:19:16,780 --> 00:19:20,000
So, these are a couple of measures of them.

356
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,200
But we care more than just about central tendency when

357
00:19:23,200 --> 00:19:24,960
evaluating risk.

358
00:19:24,960 --> 00:19:27,720
We have to do other things as well.

359
00:19:27,720 --> 00:19:31,400
And so, we want to talk about, and this is very fundamental

360
00:19:31,400 --> 00:19:33,740
to finance, we have to talk about risk.

361
00:19:33,740 --> 00:19:38,360
What could be more fundamental than risk for finance?

362
00:19:38,360 --> 00:19:39,480
So, what we have here now is the risk.

363
00:19:39,480 --> 00:19:44,040
What we have here now is a measure of variability.

364
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:50,240
And the upper equation here is something called variance.

365
00:19:50,240 --> 00:19:58,420
And it's equal to the weighted average of the x random

366
00:19:58,420 --> 00:20:02,240
variables squared deviation from the mean, weighted by

367
00:20:02,240 --> 00:20:05,040
probabilities.

368
00:20:05,040 --> 00:20:09,460
All it is, is the mean squared, the expectation of the square

369
00:20:09,460 --> 00:20:13,700
value, and the deviation from the mean.

370
00:20:13,700 --> 00:20:19,400
The mean is the center value, and the deviations from the

371
00:20:19,400 --> 00:20:25,080
mean are, whether they're positive or negative, if you

372
00:20:25,080 --> 00:20:28,220
square them, they become positive numbers.

373
00:20:28,220 --> 00:20:33,020
And so, that's called variance.

374
00:20:33,020 --> 00:20:38,140
So, for example, if x tends to be, if the return tends to be

375
00:20:38,140 --> 00:20:38,980
plus or minus 1% from the x random variables, then the x is

376
00:20:38,980 --> 00:20:41,800
1% from the mean return.

377
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:46,960
Say the mean return for an investor is 8% a year, and it's

378
00:20:46,960 --> 00:20:49,160
plus or minus 1%.

379
00:20:49,160 --> 00:20:52,060
Then you would see a lot of 1's when you'd square the

380
00:20:52,060 --> 00:20:55,900
deviation from the mean, and the variance would probably be

381
00:20:55,900 --> 00:21:00,700
1, and the standard deviation, which is, the standard

382
00:21:00,700 --> 00:21:04,700
deviation is the square root of the variance.

383
00:21:04,700 --> 00:21:05,940
And it would also be 1.

384
00:21:08,980 --> 00:21:11,540
So, this is a very simple concept.

385
00:21:11,540 --> 00:21:15,800
It's just the average squared deviation from the mean.

386
00:21:15,800 --> 00:21:20,420
The estimate of the variance, or the sample variance, is

387
00:21:20,420 --> 00:21:22,540
given by this equation.

388
00:21:22,540 --> 00:21:26,660
And it's s squared of x is just the sample mean.

389
00:21:26,660 --> 00:21:31,740
You take the deviations of the variable from its sample mean.

390
00:21:31,740 --> 00:21:33,400
You have n observations.

391
00:21:33,400 --> 00:21:35,740
Say someone has invested money for 10 years.

392
00:21:35,740 --> 00:21:37,660
You take the average return for the 10 years.

393
00:21:37,660 --> 00:21:38,860
You take the average return for the 10 years.

394
00:21:38,860 --> 00:21:41,160
And that's x bar.

395
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:46,820
And then you take all 10 deviations from the mean and

396
00:21:46,820 --> 00:21:51,300
square them, and then divide by n.

397
00:21:51,300 --> 00:21:53,300
Some people divide by n minus 1.

398
00:21:53,300 --> 00:21:55,860
But I'm just trying to be very basic and simple here, so I'm

399
00:21:55,860 --> 00:21:59,360
not going to get into these ideas.

400
00:22:05,900 --> 00:22:07,860
Then the next thing is covariance.

401
00:22:07,860 --> 00:22:08,860
And we're getting there.

402
00:22:08,860 --> 00:22:09,860
I'm just going to go through these concepts.

403
00:22:09,860 --> 00:22:11,720
They're very basic concepts.

404
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:16,620
Covariance is a measure of how two different random variables

405
00:22:16,620 --> 00:22:19,400
move together.

406
00:22:19,400 --> 00:22:23,380
So, I have two different random variables, x and y.

407
00:22:23,380 --> 00:22:30,720
So, x is the return on, let's say, the IBM Corporation.

408
00:22:30,720 --> 00:22:35,480
And y is the return on General Motors Corporation.

409
00:22:35,480 --> 00:22:37,420
And I want to know.

410
00:22:37,420 --> 00:22:38,740
When IBM comes in, what's the return on General Motors?

411
00:22:38,740 --> 00:22:42,440
When IBM goes up, does General Motors go up or not?

412
00:22:42,440 --> 00:22:48,700
So, a measure of the co-movement of the two would

413
00:22:48,700 --> 00:22:54,800
be to take the deviation of x from its mean, times the

414
00:22:54,800 --> 00:23:01,460
deviation of y from its mean, and take the average product of

415
00:23:01,460 --> 00:23:04,200
those, and that's called covariance.

416
00:23:04,200 --> 00:23:06,120
It's a positive number.

417
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:07,220
If when x is high, relative to x, it's a positive number.

418
00:23:07,220 --> 00:23:07,660
It's a positive number.

419
00:23:07,660 --> 00:23:07,900
It's a positive number.

420
00:23:07,900 --> 00:23:08,180
It's a positive number.

421
00:23:08,180 --> 00:23:08,620
It's a positive number.

422
00:23:08,620 --> 00:23:08,720
It's a positive number.

423
00:23:08,740 --> 00:23:09,040
It's a positive number.

424
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:09,380
It's a positive number.

425
00:23:09,380 --> 00:23:09,980
It's a positive number.

426
00:23:09,980 --> 00:23:10,760
It's a negative number.

427
00:23:10,760 --> 00:23:11,440
It's a positive number.

428
00:23:11,440 --> 00:23:13,580
If y tends to go up relative to its mean, y is high relative

429
00:23:13,580 --> 00:23:15,900
to its mean also.

430
00:23:15,900 --> 00:23:17,780
And it's a negative number if they tend

431
00:23:17,780 --> 00:23:20,040
to go in opposite directions.

432
00:23:20,040 --> 00:23:25,060
If GM tends to do well when IBM does poorly, then we have a

433
00:23:25,060 --> 00:23:26,720
negative covariance.

434
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:30,560
Because if one is above its mean and the other is below its

435
00:23:30,560 --> 00:23:33,600
mean, the product is going to be a negative number.

436
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:37,540
And if we get a lot of negative products like that, it means

437
00:23:37,540 --> 00:23:38,620
that they tend to move opposite each other.

438
00:23:38,620 --> 00:23:44,120
related to each other, then the covariance tends to be zero.

439
00:23:44,120 --> 00:23:47,280
And this is the core concept that I was talking about.

440
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:51,060
Some idea of unrelatedness underlies a lot of our

441
00:23:51,060 --> 00:23:53,300
thinking in risk.

442
00:23:53,300 --> 00:23:57,280
So, if X and Y are independent, they're generated

443
00:23:57,280 --> 00:24:00,840
suppose IBM's business has just nothing at all to do with

444
00:24:00,840 --> 00:24:03,920
GM's business, they're so different, then I'd say the

445
00:24:03,920 --> 00:24:06,900
covariance is probably zero.

446
00:24:06,900 --> 00:24:16,220
And then we can use that as a principle, which will underlie

447
00:24:16,220 --> 00:24:18,860
our later analysis.

448
00:24:18,860 --> 00:24:22,720
Correlation is a scaled covariance.

449
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:26,660
And it's a measure of how much two variables move together.

450
00:24:26,660 --> 00:24:30,860
But it's scaled so that it varies only over the range of

451
00:24:30,860 --> 00:24:33,380
minus one to plus one.

452
00:24:33,380 --> 00:24:36,840
So, the correlation between two random variables is

453
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:36,880
there's a correlation between two random variables.

454
00:24:36,880 --> 00:24:38,880
So, the correlation between two random variables is

455
00:24:38,880 --> 00:24:41,400
the covariance divided by the product of their standard

456
00:24:41,400 --> 00:24:42,600
deviations.

457
00:24:42,600 --> 00:24:45,660
And you can show that that always ranges between minus

458
00:24:45,660 --> 00:24:47,640
one and plus one.

459
00:24:47,640 --> 00:24:52,580
So, if two variables have a plus one correlation, that

460
00:24:52,580 --> 00:24:56,940
means they move exactly together.

461
00:24:56,940 --> 00:25:01,640
When one moves up 5%, the other one moves up 5% exactly.

462
00:25:01,640 --> 00:25:05,560
If they have a correlation of minus one, it means they move

463
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:06,820
exactly opposite each other.

464
00:25:06,820 --> 00:25:09,440
These things don't happen very often in finance, but in

465
00:25:09,440 --> 00:25:10,860
theory, that's what happens.

466
00:25:10,860 --> 00:25:13,740
If they have a zero correlation, that means there's

467
00:25:13,740 --> 00:25:18,820
no tendency for them to move together at all.

468
00:25:18,820 --> 00:25:21,820
If two variables are independent, then their

469
00:25:21,820 --> 00:25:23,820
correlation should be zero.

470
00:25:31,820 --> 00:25:36,760
The variance of the sum of two random variables is the number

471
00:25:36,760 --> 00:25:37,760
of variables.

472
00:25:37,760 --> 00:25:41,760
So, the variance of the first random variable plus the

473
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:45,260
variance of the second random variable plus twice the

474
00:25:45,260 --> 00:25:49,000
covariance of the random variables.

475
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,760
So, if the two random variables are independent of

476
00:25:51,760 --> 00:25:55,260
each other, then their covariance is zero, and then

477
00:25:55,260 --> 00:25:59,520
the variance of the sum is the sum of the variances.

478
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:01,760
But that is not necessary.

479
00:26:01,760 --> 00:26:06,700
That's true if the random variables are independent, but

480
00:26:06,700 --> 00:26:08,700
if they are, then they are not.

481
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:10,700
But we're going to see that breakdown of independence is

482
00:26:10,700 --> 00:26:12,700
the story of this lecture right now.

483
00:26:12,700 --> 00:26:19,700
And I don't want to, we want to think about independence as

484
00:26:19,700 --> 00:26:21,700
mattering a lot.

485
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:24,700
And it's a model or a core idea.

486
00:26:24,700 --> 00:26:26,700
But when do we know that things are independent?

487
00:26:31,700 --> 00:26:32,700
This is a plot.

488
00:26:32,700 --> 00:26:35,700
I was telling you earlier about the, I don't know, the

489
00:26:36,700 --> 00:26:38,700
decolonization of the stock market.

490
00:26:38,700 --> 00:26:39,700
Let me see.

491
00:26:43,700 --> 00:26:45,700
OK.

492
00:26:45,700 --> 00:26:47,700
Let me just hold off on that a minute.

493
00:26:56,700 --> 00:26:57,700
Well, I'll tell you what that was.

494
00:26:57,700 --> 00:27:02,700
That was a plot of the stock market since, from 2000 to 2010

495
00:27:02,700 --> 00:27:03,700
in the U.S.

496
00:27:03,700 --> 00:27:05,700
And I'm going to come back to that.

497
00:27:05,700 --> 00:27:09,700
But this is the decline in the stock market from 2000 to

498
00:27:09,700 --> 00:27:11,700
2002 or 2003.

499
00:27:11,700 --> 00:27:17,700
And this is the more recent decline from 2007 to 2009.

500
00:27:17,700 --> 00:27:24,700
Those are the cumulative effect of a lot of little shocks that

501
00:27:24,700 --> 00:27:25,700
didn't happen all at once.

502
00:27:25,700 --> 00:27:27,700
It happened over years.

503
00:27:27,700 --> 00:27:30,700
And we want to think about the probability of those shocks

504
00:27:30,700 --> 00:27:32,700
occurring.

505
00:27:32,700 --> 00:27:34,700
And that's all.

506
00:27:34,700 --> 00:27:40,700
That's where I am going.

507
00:27:40,700 --> 00:27:51,700
But what I want to talk about is the core concept of

508
00:27:51,700 --> 00:28:02,700
independence leading to some basic principles of risk

509
00:28:02,700 --> 00:28:03,700
management.

510
00:28:03,700 --> 00:28:05,700
And that's what I'm going to talk about in a moment.

511
00:28:05,700 --> 00:28:10,700
The crisis that we've seen here in the stock market is the

512
00:28:10,700 --> 00:28:13,700
accumulation of, you see all these ups and downs in the

513
00:28:13,700 --> 00:28:16,700
stock market?

514
00:28:16,700 --> 00:28:22,700
And then all these ups and downs on the way up.

515
00:28:22,700 --> 00:28:26,700
There were relatively more downs in the period from 2000 to

516
00:28:26,700 --> 00:28:27,700
2002.

517
00:28:27,700 --> 00:28:31,700
And there were relatively more ups from the period 2003 to 2006.

518
00:28:31,700 --> 00:28:34,700
But how do we understand the cumulative effect of that,

519
00:28:34,700 --> 00:28:38,700
which is what matters?

520
00:28:38,700 --> 00:28:44,700
So, we have to have some kind of probability model.

521
00:28:44,700 --> 00:28:48,700
The question immediately is, are these shocks that affected

522
00:28:48,700 --> 00:28:52,700
the stock market, are they independent, or are they

523
00:28:52,700 --> 00:28:53,700
independent?

524
00:28:53,700 --> 00:28:55,700
Or are they independent?

525
00:28:55,700 --> 00:28:57,700
Or are they independent?

526
00:28:57,700 --> 00:28:59,700
Or are they independent?

527
00:28:59,700 --> 00:29:05,700
Are they somehow related to each other?

528
00:29:05,700 --> 00:29:11,700
And that is the core question that made it so difficult for us

529
00:29:11,700 --> 00:29:15,700
to understand how to deal with the potential of such a crisis

530
00:29:15,700 --> 00:29:20,700
and why so many people got in trouble dealing with this

531
00:29:20,700 --> 00:29:21,700
crisis.

532
00:29:21,700 --> 00:29:25,700
So, we have to understand that the crisis is not just a

533
00:29:25,700 --> 00:29:26,700
crisis.

534
00:29:26,700 --> 00:29:27,700
It's a crisis.

535
00:29:27,700 --> 00:29:28,700
It's a crisis.

536
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:37,700
And we had a big financial crisis in the United States in

537
00:29:37,700 --> 00:29:41,700
1987, when there was a stock market crash that was bigger

538
00:29:41,700 --> 00:29:43,700
than any before in one day.

539
00:29:43,700 --> 00:29:46,700
We'll be talking about that.

540
00:29:46,700 --> 00:29:56,700
But after the 1987 crash, companies started to compute a

541
00:29:56,700 --> 00:30:03,700
measure of the risk to their company, which is called value

542
00:30:03,700 --> 00:30:05,700
at risk.

543
00:30:05,700 --> 00:30:08,700
I'll write it up like that.

544
00:30:08,700 --> 00:30:12,700
I capitalized the first and the last letter, so you'll know that

545
00:30:12,700 --> 00:30:14,700
this is not the same thing as variance.

546
00:30:14,700 --> 00:30:23,700
This is value at risk.

547
00:30:23,700 --> 00:30:28,700
And what companies would do after 1987 to try to measure the

548
00:30:28,700 --> 00:30:36,700
risk of their activities is to compute a number, something like

549
00:30:36,700 --> 00:30:37,700
this.

550
00:30:37,700 --> 00:30:44,700
They would say, there's a 5% probability that we will lose

551
00:30:44,700 --> 00:30:51,700
$10 million in a year.

552
00:30:51,700 --> 00:30:52,700
That's the kind of risk.

553
00:30:52,700 --> 00:30:56,700
That's the kind of bottom line that value at risk calculations

554
00:30:56,700 --> 00:30:57,700
would make.

555
00:31:01,700 --> 00:31:05,700
And so, you need a probability model to make these

556
00:31:05,700 --> 00:31:08,700
calculations.

557
00:31:08,700 --> 00:31:15,700
And so, you need probability theory in order to do that.

558
00:31:15,700 --> 00:31:21,700
Many companies had calculated value at risk numbers like

559
00:31:21,700 --> 00:31:22,700
this.

560
00:31:22,700 --> 00:31:26,700
And so, they did this and told their investors, we can't do too

561
00:31:26,700 --> 00:31:30,700
badly, because there's no way that we could lose.

562
00:31:30,700 --> 00:31:33,700
The probability is only 5% that we could lose 10 million.

563
00:31:33,700 --> 00:31:35,700
And they have other numbers like this.

564
00:31:35,700 --> 00:31:40,700
But they were implicitly making assumptions about independence,

565
00:31:40,700 --> 00:31:43,700
or at least relative independence.

566
00:31:43,700 --> 00:31:51,700
And that's the concept I'm trying to emphasize here.

567
00:31:51,700 --> 00:32:00,700
There's a core concept in finance, and it's not one that

568
00:32:00,700 --> 00:32:04,700
is easy to be precise about.

569
00:32:04,700 --> 00:32:09,700
We have an intuitive idea that we see the ups and downs on the

570
00:32:09,700 --> 00:32:12,700
stock market, and we've noticed them, and they all average out

571
00:32:12,700 --> 00:32:15,700
to something not too bad.

572
00:32:15,700 --> 00:32:19,700
The problem is, that brought us this crisis, is that the value

573
00:32:19,700 --> 00:32:22,700
of the stock market is not the same.

574
00:32:22,700 --> 00:32:24,700
And so, the people who made these calculations were too

575
00:32:24,700 --> 00:32:26,700
optimistic.

576
00:32:26,700 --> 00:32:30,700
Companies all over the world were estimating very small

577
00:32:30,700 --> 00:32:34,700
numbers here relative to what actually happened.

578
00:32:34,700 --> 00:32:39,700
And that's a problem.

579
00:32:39,700 --> 00:32:42,700
I want to emphasize core concepts here, intuitive

580
00:32:42,700 --> 00:32:48,700
concepts that you probably already have.

581
00:32:48,700 --> 00:32:51,700
And I want to call the law of large numbers.

582
00:33:00,700 --> 00:33:04,700
And the law of large numbers says that, and there's many

583
00:33:04,700 --> 00:33:06,700
different ways of formulating it, but putting it in its

584
00:33:06,700 --> 00:33:12,700
simplest form, that if I have a lot of independent shocks and

585
00:33:12,700 --> 00:33:15,700
average them out, on average, there's not going to be much

586
00:33:15,700 --> 00:33:17,700
uncertainty.

587
00:33:17,700 --> 00:33:22,700
So, let's say I flip a coin once.

588
00:33:22,700 --> 00:33:26,700
And let's say I'm making a bet, plus or minus, if it comes up

589
00:33:26,700 --> 00:33:30,700
heads, I'll win $1, if it comes up tails, I'll lose $1.

590
00:33:30,700 --> 00:33:35,700
Well, I have a risk, I mean, I have a standard deviation of $1

591
00:33:35,700 --> 00:33:38,700
in my outcome for that.

592
00:33:38,700 --> 00:33:44,700
But if I do it 100 times, and average the result, there's not

593
00:33:44,700 --> 00:33:45,700
going to be much risk at all.

594
00:33:45,700 --> 00:33:46,700
And that's the law of large numbers. It says, you can do it, you can do it, you can do it.

595
00:33:46,700 --> 00:33:53,260
It says that the variance of the average of n random

596
00:33:53,260 --> 00:33:56,060
variables that are all independent and identically

597
00:33:56,060 --> 00:34:01,020
distributed goes to zero as the number of elements in the

598
00:34:01,020 --> 00:34:03,760
average goes to infinity.

599
00:34:03,760 --> 00:34:12,420
And so, that's a fundamental concept that underlies both

600
00:34:12,420 --> 00:34:14,520
finance and insurance.

601
00:34:14,520 --> 00:34:22,280
The idea that tossing a coin or throwing a die has

602
00:34:22,280 --> 00:34:25,400
uncertainty in a small number of observations, but the

603
00:34:25,400 --> 00:34:29,040
uncertainty vanishes in a large number of observations

604
00:34:29,040 --> 00:34:30,540
goes back to the ancient world.

605
00:34:30,540 --> 00:34:33,640
Aristotle made this observation.

606
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:35,400
But he didn't have probability theory, and he

607
00:34:35,400 --> 00:34:36,680
couldn't carry it further.

608
00:34:39,980 --> 00:34:43,380
So, the idea of insurance, the fundamental concept of

609
00:34:43,380 --> 00:34:44,520
insurance is this.

610
00:34:44,520 --> 00:34:48,840
It relies on this intuitive idea.

611
00:34:48,840 --> 00:34:52,580
And the idea was intuitive enough that insurance was known

612
00:34:52,580 --> 00:34:55,560
and practiced in ancient times.

613
00:34:55,560 --> 00:35:02,600
But the insurance concept depends on independence.

614
00:35:02,600 --> 00:35:10,460
And so, the independence is something that apparently

615
00:35:10,460 --> 00:35:12,680
breaks down at times like these.

616
00:35:12,680 --> 00:35:14,280
Like these big, big, big, big, big, big, big, big, big, big,

617
00:35:14,280 --> 00:35:20,600
big, big, down crises that we've seen in the stock market

618
00:35:20,600 --> 00:35:23,460
in the two episodes in the beginning of the 20th century.

619
00:35:25,540 --> 00:35:31,240
So, the law of large numbers has to do with the idea that

620
00:35:31,240 --> 00:35:39,760
if I have a large number of random variables, what is the

621
00:35:39,760 --> 00:35:42,100
variance?

622
00:35:42,100 --> 00:35:44,240
The variance of the value of a coin is as if the value of a

623
00:35:44,240 --> 00:35:51,280
x1 plus x2 plus x3 up to xn.

624
00:35:55,400 --> 00:35:57,620
If they're all independent, then all of the

625
00:35:57,620 --> 00:35:59,420
covariances are zero.

626
00:35:59,420 --> 00:36:08,080
So, that equals the variance of x1 plus the variance of x2

627
00:36:08,080 --> 00:36:10,640
plus the variance of xn.

628
00:36:10,640 --> 00:36:14,040
There's n terms, I'm not showing them all.

629
00:36:14,040 --> 00:36:16,440
OK?

630
00:36:16,440 --> 00:36:21,040
So, if they all have the same variance, then the variance of

631
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:26,840
the sum of n of them is n times their variance.

632
00:36:26,840 --> 00:36:30,220
And that means the standard deviation, which is the square

633
00:36:30,220 --> 00:36:34,320
root of the variance, is equal to the square root of n times

634
00:36:34,320 --> 00:36:37,580
the standard deviation of one of them.

635
00:36:37,580 --> 00:36:40,840
The mean is divided by n.

636
00:36:40,840 --> 00:36:43,880
So, that means that the standard deviation of the mean

637
00:36:43,880 --> 00:36:44,020
is equal to the standard deviation of n times the

638
00:36:44,020 --> 00:36:45,020
square root of n.

639
00:36:45,020 --> 00:36:47,020
So, the mean is equal to the standard deviation of one of

640
00:36:47,020 --> 00:36:50,020
the x's divided by the square root of n.

641
00:36:50,020 --> 00:36:54,420
So, as n goes large, you can see that the standard

642
00:36:54,420 --> 00:36:57,220
deviation of the mean goes to zero.

643
00:36:57,220 --> 00:37:00,020
And that's the law of large numbers.

644
00:37:00,020 --> 00:37:02,140
OK?

645
00:37:02,140 --> 00:37:06,760
But the problem is, so you can look at a financial firm, and

646
00:37:06,760 --> 00:37:09,380
they have returns for a number of years.

647
00:37:09,380 --> 00:37:12,900
And those returns can be accumulated to give some sense

648
00:37:12,900 --> 00:37:13,860
of their total return.

649
00:37:13,860 --> 00:37:17,860
So, the question is, what's the total outcome?

650
00:37:17,860 --> 00:37:23,860
But does the total outcome really behave properly?

651
00:37:23,860 --> 00:37:28,860
Does it become certain over a longer interval of time?

652
00:37:28,860 --> 00:37:34,860
Well, apparently not, because of the possibility that the

653
00:37:34,860 --> 00:37:37,860
observations are not independent.

654
00:37:37,860 --> 00:37:42,860
So, we want to move from analysis of variance to

655
00:37:42,860 --> 00:37:43,860
probability.

656
00:37:43,860 --> 00:37:50,860
So, let's say that VAR came in in 1987, or thereabouts, after

657
00:37:50,860 --> 00:37:53,860
the stock market crash of 87.

658
00:37:53,860 --> 00:37:59,860
There's a new idea coming up now after the recent crisis,

659
00:37:59,860 --> 00:38:05,860
and it's called CoVAR.

660
00:38:05,860 --> 00:38:10,860
And this is a concept emphasized by Professor

661
00:38:10,860 --> 00:38:12,700
Brunnermeier.

662
00:38:12,700 --> 00:38:18,700
He's a professor at Princeton and some of his colleagues.

663
00:38:18,700 --> 00:38:24,700
That we have to change value at risk to recognize that

664
00:38:24,700 --> 00:38:31,700
portfolios can sometimes co-vary more than we thought, that

665
00:38:31,700 --> 00:38:34,700
there might be episodes when everything goes wrong at the

666
00:38:34,700 --> 00:38:35,700
same time.

667
00:38:35,700 --> 00:38:40,700
So, suddenly the covariance goes up.

668
00:38:40,700 --> 00:38:46,700
So, CoVAR is an alternative to value at risk that does

669
00:38:46,700 --> 00:38:51,700
different kinds of calculations in the present environment.

670
00:38:51,700 --> 00:38:54,700
I think we recognize the need for that.

